   QUICK START — NEW INSTALLATION
   ============================================================================

   1. Create a QFront directory:
        MD C:\QFRONT

   2. Copy all QFront files to C:\QFRONT.

   3. Create your FidoNet directory structure:
        MD C:\FIDO
        MD C:\FIDO\OUTBOUND        (BSO outbound — pending mail)
        MD C:\FIDO\INBOUND         (received files land here)
        MD C:\FIDO\NETMAIL         (netmail .MSG storage)
        MD C:\FIDO\NODELIST        (raw nodelists)

   4. Run QFCONFIG.EXE to create your configuration:
        CD C:\QFRONT
        QFCONFIG

      Set at minimum:
        - Your FidoNet address (e.g. 1:234/56)
        - Outbound directory (C:\FIDO\OUTBOUND)
        - Inbound directory (C:\FIDO\INBOUND)
        - NetMail directory (C:\FIDO\NETMAIL)
        - COM port and baud rate
        - Modem init string

   5. Place your nodelist in C:\FIDO\NODELIST and compile it:
        QNLIST /COMPILE

   6. Set up BOARD.BAT (see next section).

   7. Run BOARD.BAT to start your BBS:
        BOARD


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 5

   QUICK START — UPGRADING FROM ORIGINAL QFRONT
   ============================================================================

   If you are running the original QFront v1.20a for Wildcat!, you must
   create a new qfront.cfg file. The original used binary configuration
   files (QFCONFIG.DAT) which are not compatible.

   Your BSO outbound directory structure, nodelists, and netmail are all
   compatible — FTS-5005 is the same standard. You only need to:

   1. Back up your original QFront directory.

   2. Copy the new QFront files over the old ones.

   3. Run QFCONFIG.EXE to create a new qfront.cfg with your settings.
      Re-enter your addresses, paths, modem strings, events, and areas.

   4. Recompile your nodelist:
        QNLIST /COMPILE

   5. Update BOARD.BAT if your paths changed.

   Your .PKT files, flow files (.?lo, .?ut), and netmail .MSG files will
   work without modification.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 6

   BOARD.BAT SETUP
   ============================================================================

   BOARD.BAT is the master batch file that runs your BBS. It loops between
   QFront (waiting for calls) and PCBoard (handling callers). QFront exits
   with an errorlevel that tells BOARD.BAT what to do next.

   IMPORTANT: DOS checks errorlevels with "IF ERRORLEVEL n" which means
   "if errorlevel is n OR HIGHER". You must check from highest to lowest.


   Example BOARD.BAT:

     @ECHO OFF
     CD \QFRONT

     :LOOP
     QFRONT.EXE qfront.cfg
     IF ERRORLEVEL 5 GOTO FAX
     IF ERRORLEVEL 1 GOTO BBS
     GOTO LOOP

     :BBS
     CD \PCB
     PCBOARD.EXE
     CD \QFRONT
     GOTO LOOP

     :FAX
     CD \FAX
     FAXRECV.EXE
     CD \QFRONT
     GOTO LOOP


   Errorlevel summary:

     0   Normal exit (outbound scan completed, no call) — loop back
     1   Human caller detected — load PCBoard
     5   FAX call detected — load FAX software

   When QFront exits with errorlevel 1, the modem carrier is still up.
   PCBoard opens the same COM port and finds a connected caller waiting.
   This handoff is seamless — the caller sees the BBS login prompt without
   any delay or disconnection.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 7

   BOARD.BAT — MULTI-NODE SETUP
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   For multi-node systems, create a separate BOARD.BAT for each node with
   node-specific config files:

     :LOOP
     QFRONT.EXE NODE1.CFG
     IF ERRORLEVEL 5 GOTO FAX
     IF ERRORLEVEL 1 GOTO BBS
     GOTO LOOP

     :BBS
     CD \PCB
     PCBOARD.EXE /NODE:1
     CD \QFRONT
     GOTO LOOP

   Each node needs:
     - Its own qfront.cfg (or NODE1.CFG, NODE2.CFG, etc.)
     - Its own COM port setting
     - Shared outbound/inbound directories (QFront uses .BSY locks to
       prevent conflicts between nodes)


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 8

   CONFIGURATION — BASIC SETTINGS
   ============================================================================

   The configuration file (qfront.cfg) is plain text. Each line contains
   one setting in KEY=VALUE format. Lines starting with # or ; are comments.
   Section headers like [Event.ZMH] define event schedules.

   Example minimal qfront.cfg:

     # QFront Configuration
     Address=1:234/56
     Outbound=C:\FIDO\OUTBOUND
     Inbound=C:\FIDO\INBOUND
     NetmailDir=C:\FIDO\NETMAIL
     LogFile=C:\QFRONT\QFRONT.LOG
     Mailer=C:\BINKD\BINKD.EXE
     Tosser=C:\QFRONT\QSCAN.EXE /BOTH
     NodelistDir=C:\FIDO\NODELIST
     NodelistBase=NODELIST
     ComPort=1
     BaudRate=115200
     MaxRetries=5
     RetryDelay=300
     Debug=0


   SETTINGS REFERENCE
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Address=zone:net/node[.point]
     Your FidoNet address. You can have up to 16 AKA (Also Known As)
     addresses by specifying multiple Address= lines.
     Example: Address=1:234/56
              Address=2:5020/1234

   Debug=0|1
     Enable verbose debug logging. Set to 1 for troubleshooting, 0 for
     normal operation. Debug output goes to the log file and stderr.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 9

   CONFIGURATION — PATHS AND DIRECTORIES
   ============================================================================

   Outbound=path
     BSO outbound directory root. This is where QFront looks for pending
     mail to send. Flow files (.?lo), netmail packets (.?ut), file
     requests (.req), and lock files (.bsy) live here.

     For multi-zone operation, QFront creates subdirectories:
       OUTBOUND\          (your primary zone)
       OUTBOUND.002\      (zone 2, in hex)
       OUTBOUND.003\      (zone 3)

   Inbound=path
     Directory where received files are stored. Incoming .PKT files,
     file attaches, TIC files, and any other received files go here.

   TempInbound=path
     Optional. Directory for files received during non-password-protected
     sessions. If not set, all files go to Inbound.

   NetmailDir=path
     Directory for netmail storage in .MSG format (FTS-0001 Type 2).
     Each message is a separate numbered file (1.MSG, 2.MSG, etc.).

   LogFile=path
     Path to the QFront log file. All session activity, errors, and
     debug messages are written here. The log is appended, not overwritten.

     Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS [LVL] message
     Levels: DBG (debug), "   " (info), WRN (warning), ERR (error)


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 10

   CONFIGURATION — MODEM / SERIAL PORT
   ============================================================================

   ComPort=n
     COM port number (1-4). Default: 1.

   BaudRate=n
   LockedBaud=n
     DTE (locked) baud rate. This is the speed between your computer and
     the modem, NOT the connect speed. The modem negotiates DCE speed
     independently. Set this to your modem's maximum DTE rate.
     Common values: 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, 115200.
     Default: 115200.

   The modem init strings, answer command, hangup command, and dial
   prefix/suffix are configured through QFCONFIG or set directly in the
   MdmConfig structure (see source for details).

   Default modem commands:
     Init string:    ATZ          (reset modem to factory defaults)
     Answer command: ATA          (answer incoming call)
     Hangup command: ATH0         (hang up)
     Dial prefix:    ATDT         (tone dial)

   QFront uses two methods to hang up the modem:
     1. Drop DTR for 1 second (preferred, requires AT&D2 on modem)
     2. Send +++ escape then ATH0 (fallback, works with any modem)
   Both methods are always used for maximum compatibility.

   Serial port access methods (auto-detected):
     DOS:   FOSSIL driver (INT 14h) if available, else direct 16550 UART
     Win32: CreateFile on \\.\COMn
     Linux: /dev/ttyS(n-1) or /dev/ttyUSB(n-1) via termios


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 11

   CONFIGURATION — SESSION SETTINGS
   ============================================================================

   MaxRetries=n
     Maximum number of times to attempt calling a node per day before
     marking it as undialable. Default: 5.

     After MaxRetries consecutive failures, the node is placed on hold
     for HoldTime seconds. After 3 days of continuous failure, the node
     is marked permanently undialable until the sysop clears it.

   RetryDelay=n
     Seconds to wait between successive calls to the same node.
     Default: 300 (5 minutes).

     This prevents hammering a busy node. Between retries, QFront
     processes other nodes in the outbound queue and checks for
     incoming calls.

   HoldTime=n
     Seconds to hold a node after it exceeds MaxRetries. Default: 3600
     (1 hour). After the hold expires, QFront will try calling again.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 12

   CONFIGURATION — EXTERNAL PROGRAMS
   ============================================================================

   Mailer=path
     Path to external mailer program (typically binkd). Used for BinkP/IP
     sessions. QFront calls this with:
       MAILER -p -P zone:net/node [-h hostname]

     For modem-only operation, leave this blank and QFront will use its
     built-in EMSI/YooHoo protocols.

   Tosser=path
     Path to external mail tosser. Called after successful sessions to
     process incoming .PKT files. Typically:
       Tosser=C:\QFRONT\QSCAN.EXE /BOTH

     You can also use third-party tossers like HPT, Squish, etc.

   TicProc=path
     Path to TIC file processor. Called after sessions to process incoming
     .TIC files (file area distributions). If not set, QFront logs TIC
     files but does not process them.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 13

   CONFIGURATION — NODELIST
   ============================================================================

   NodelistDir=path
     Directory containing raw nodelist files. QFront looks here for the
     compiled .NDX index.

   NodelistBase=name
     Base name of the primary nodelist (max 8 chars). Default: NODELIST.
     QFront looks for NodelistDir\NodelistBase (no extension) and the
     compiled index at NodelistDir\NodelistBase.NDX.

   The nodelist is used for:
     - Looking up phone numbers and hostnames for dialing
     - Checking node status (Down, Hold, CM flags)
     - Finding BinkP-capable nodes (IBN flag)
     - Routing decisions (net host lookup)

   To compile your nodelist after receiving a new one or a nodediff:
     QNLIST /COMPILE          Compile unconditionally
     QNLIST /COMPILENEW       Compile only if new diffs found


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 14

   CONFIGURATION — SEMAPHORE FILES
   ============================================================================

   Semaphore files are a simple way for external programs to signal QFront.
   QFront checks for these files periodically. When found, the file is
   deleted and QFront exits with the specified errorlevel.

   Configuration:
     Semaphore=path:errorlevel

   Examples:
     Semaphore=C:\FIDO\QFEXIT.NOW:0       Exit normally
     Semaphore=C:\FIDO\SCANMAIL.NOW:3      Exit with errorlevel 3
     Semaphore=C:\FIDO\COMPNL.NOW:4        Exit with errorlevel 4

   BOARD.BAT can then check these errorlevels:
     IF ERRORLEVEL 4 GOTO COMPILE_NL
     IF ERRORLEVEL 3 GOTO SCAN_MAIL

   This mechanism lets PCBoard doors, batch files, or other programs
   tell QFront to reload, rescan, compile nodelists, or shut down.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 15

   CONFIGURATION — EVENT SCHEDULING
   ============================================================================

   Events are time-based actions defined as [Event.NAME] sections in
   qfront.cfg. Each event has a schedule (days and times), flags that
   control behavior, and optional actions (run batch file, exit with
   errorlevel).

   Example:

     [Event.ZMH]
     Description=Zone Mail Hour
     Days=Daily
     Start=01:00
     End=05:00
     ForcePoll=yes
     SendCMOnly=yes
     EndNoMail=yes

     [Event.NightPoll]
     Description=Nightly poll run
     Days=MTWTF
     Start=23:00
     End=06:00
     ForcePoll=yes
     ListedOnly=yes

   Schedule settings:
     Description=text         Human-readable description
     Days=pattern             When to run (see below)
     Start=HH:MM              Start time
     End=HH:MM                End time
     Active=yes|no            Enable/disable without deleting

   Day patterns:
     Daily                    Every day
     Weekdays                 Monday through Friday
     Weekends                 Saturday and Sunday
     sMTWtFS                  Individual days (s=Sun, S=Sat, t=Thu)


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 16

   EVENT FLAGS
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Event flags control what QFront does during the event window.
   Set them as Flag=yes in the event section.

   Session control:
     ReceiveOnly       Don't dial out — only receive incoming calls
     SendCMOnly        Only dial Continuous Mail (24-hour) nodes
     SendNonCMOnly     Only dial non-CM nodes
     ListedOnly        Only dial nodes found in the nodelist
     ForcePoll         Force outbound poll regardless of restrictions
     PollDuring        Allow polling during event window

   Mail handling:
     EchoOnly          Process EchoMail only (ignore NetMail)
     NetOnly           Process NetMail only (ignore EchoMail)
     NoHeldAttach      Don't process held file attaches

   Pre/post actions:
     ScanBefore        Run tosser before event starts
     CompileNodelist   Compile nodelist after event
     CompileOnReceive  Compile if new nodelist was received
     Rescan            Rescan areas after event
     SlideTime         Slide start time if still processing

   Exit conditions:
     EndNoMail         End event when outbound queue is empty
     ExitNoMail        Exit program when queue is empty
     ExitWhenDone      Exit after processing all outbound

   Exit:
     Errorlevel=n      Exit code for ExitNoMail/ExitWhenDone

   Auto-poll:
     AutoPoll=addr,addr,...   Addresses to poll during this event


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 17

   CONFIGURATION — ECHOMAIL AREAS
   ============================================================================

   EchoMail areas are defined as [Area.TAG] sections in qfront.cfg.

   Example:

     [Area.NET_DEV]
     Path=C:\FIDO\AREAS\NET_DEV
     Conference=42
     Passthrough=N

     [Area.FIDONEWS]
     Path=C:\FIDO\AREAS\FIDONEWS
     Conference=15
     Passthrough=N

   Settings:
     Path=dir            Message base directory (.MSG format)
     Conference=n        PCBoard conference number
     Passthrough=Y|N     Pass-through area (forward without storing)


   CONFIGURATION — ROUTING RULES
   ============================================================================

   Routing rules tell QFront how to send mail for each destination address.
   Rules are processed in order; first match wins.

   Config syntax:
     Route zone:net/node direct       Call the node directly
     Route zone:net/* via zone:net/0  Route through net host
     Route zone:net/* hub zone:net/n  Route through hub
     Route zone:net/* hold            Hold for pickup
     NoPoll zone:net/node             Never dial this node

   Wildcards: * matches any value at that position.

   Examples:
     Route 1:234/56 direct            Direct call to 1:234/56
     Route 1:234/* via 1:234/0        All net 234 via the host
     Route 2:* via 2:5020/0           All zone 2 via 2:5020/0
     NoPoll 1:999/1                   Never poll this node


