   COMMAND LINE OPTIONS
   ============================================================================

   QFront supports both Unix-style and DOS-style command line options.

   QFRONT.EXE [options]

   Unix-style:
     -c <config>     Specify config file (default: qfront.cfg)
     -p <addr>       Poll a specific node and exit
     -s              Single pass (scan once, don't loop)
     -d              Debug mode (verbose logging)
     -h              Show help

   DOS-style:
     /C<config>      Specify config file (e.g. /CNODE1.CFG)
     /DEBUG          Create verbose debug log
     /LOCALONLY      Local-only mode (no modem, outbound only)
     /NOANSWER       Don't auto-answer incoming calls
     /NOCLEARWC      Don't clear NODEINFO.DAT on startup
     /NO16550        Disable 16550 UART FIFO
     /NOMOUSE        Disable mouse support
     /COLOR          Force color display
     /MONO           Force monochrome display

   Examples:
     QFRONT                       Run with qfront.cfg, full loop
     QFRONT -c node2.cfg          Run with node2.cfg
     QFRONT /CNODE2.CFG           Same, DOS style
     QFRONT -p 1:234/56           Poll node 1:234/56 and exit
     QFRONT -s -d                 Single pass with debug
     QFRONT /DEBUG /NOANSWER      Debug mode, no auto-answer


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 20

   QSCAN COMMAND LINE OPTIONS
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QSCAN.EXE [options]

     /TOSS           Toss inbound .PKT files to message base
     /SCAN           Scan message base for outbound echomail
     /BOTH           Toss + scan
     /NETMAIL        Process netmail only
     /AREA:tag       Process a specific area only
     /RESET          Reset high message pointers
     /SETHIGH        Set pointers to current high
     /FORCE          Force rescan
     /DEBUG          Verbose output

   QNLIST COMMAND LINE OPTIONS
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QNLIST.EXE [options]

     /COMPILE        Compile primary + private nodelists
     /COMPILENEW     Compile only if new diffs found
     /C<config>      Specify config file

   QFUTIL COMMAND LINE OPTIONS
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QFUTIL.EXE [options]

     /POLL /ADDR:addr                    Create poll
     /NETMAIL /ADDR:addr /TO:name        Create netmail
     /FREQ /ADDR:addr /FILE:name         File request
     /UREQUEST /ADDR:addr /FILE:name     Update request
     /FORWARD /ADDR:addr /TO:name        Forward netmail

   Optional flags:
     /FROM:name      Sender name
     /SUBJ:text      Subject line
     /FILE:path      File to attach
     /FLAGS:flags    Message flags (PVT, CRA, K/S, HLD)
     /PWRD:pwd       File request password


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 21

   ERRORLEVELS
   ============================================================================

   QFront exits with these errorlevels. BOARD.BAT should check them from
   highest to lowest (DOS "IF ERRORLEVEL n" means >= n).

   Errorlevel   Meaning                    BOARD.BAT action
   ----------   -------------------------  --------------------------
   0            Normal exit, no call       Loop back to QFront
   1            Human caller detected      Load PCBOARD.EXE
   5            FAX call detected          Load FAX software
   3+           Semaphore/event triggered  Custom (compile NL, etc.)

   When QFront returns errorlevel 1:
     - The modem carrier is STILL UP (connected)
     - QFront closed its handle to the COM port
     - PCBoard opens the same COM port and finds a caller waiting
     - The handoff is seamless — no disconnect, no reconnect

   When QFront returns errorlevel 5:
     - The modem has been hung up (FAX calls can't be converted)
     - FAX software can reinitialize the modem for the next call

   When QFront returns errorlevel 0:
     - Normal timeout — no call came in during the wait period
     - Or: outbound scan completed, single-pass mode
     - Loop back to QFront to wait for the next call


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 22

   INBOUND CALL HANDLING
   ============================================================================

   When a call comes in, QFront follows this sequence:

   1. RING DETECTION
      QFront monitors the modem for "RING" responses. Other unsolicited
      modem responses (Caller ID data, OK from previous commands) are
      ignored.

   2. ANSWER
      QFront sends the answer command (ATA) to the modem. The modem
      negotiates with the remote modem and reports:
        CONNECT xxxxx  — data call at xxxxx bps (proceed to step 3)
        CONNECT FAX    — fax call (exit with errorlevel 5)
        NO CARRIER     — caller hung up (return to waiting)

   3. HANDSHAKE DETECTION (20-second window)
      QFront listens for a FidoNet handshake from the remote system:

        **EMSI_INQ     — EMSI mailer (FSC-0056). Modern standard.
        ENQ (0x05)     — YooHoo/WaZOO mailer (FTS-0006). Older standard.
        TSYNC (0xAE)   — FTS-0001 basic session. Rarely used after ~1990.

      If a handshake is detected within 20 seconds, QFront runs a
      FidoNet session (step 4).

      If NO handshake is detected (timeout), the caller is assumed to be
      a human with a terminal program. QFront exits with errorlevel 1
      and BOARD.BAT loads PCBoard.

      During this 20-second window, human callers see garbage characters
      on their screen (the EMSI_INQ attempts from the remote end, or
      nothing at all). This is normal. Once PCBoard loads, they see the
      login prompt.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 23

   4. FIDONET SESSION
      After handshake detection, QFront completes the FidoNet session:

      a. WaZOO handshake — exchange addresses, system names, passwords.
         QFront sends our address and capabilities; the remote sends theirs.

      b. Receive inbound files — the calling system sends .PKT packets,
         file attaches, and TIC files via Zmodem batch transfer.

      c. Send outbound files — if we have pending mail for the calling
         system's address, we send it via Zmodem.

      d. Hang up — both sides send ZFIN and disconnect.

   5. POST-SESSION
      After a FidoNet session completes:
        - The tosser is called (if configured) to process inbound .PKT
          files into the message base
        - TIC processor is called (if configured) to process file area
          distributions
        - QFront returns to waiting for the next call


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 24

   OUTBOUND MAIL FLOW
   ============================================================================

   Between incoming calls, QFront scans the BSO outbound directory for
   pending mail to send.

   BSO DIRECTORY STRUCTURE (FTS-5005)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The outbound directory contains files named NNNNNNNN.ext where:
     NNNNNNNN = destination net (4 hex digits) + node (4 hex digits)
     ext      = flavour + type

   File types:
     .iut .cut .dut .fut .hut    Netmail packet (by flavour)
     .ilo .clo .dlo .flo .hlo    File reference list (by flavour)
     .req                         File request
     .bsy                         Busy lock (node being called)
     .hld                         Hold file (node on hold)
     .try                         Session attempt history

   Flavour priority (highest first):
     i = Immediate    Ignore all restrictions, call now
     c = Continuous   Ignore external restrictions
     d = Direct       Respect all restrictions
     f = Normal       May be rerouted through hub
     h = Hold         Wait for remote to call us

   Flow files (.?lo) contain one file path per line:
     C:\FIDO\OUTBOUND\PACKET.PKT      Send this file
     ^C:\TEMP\TEMPFILE.ZIP             Send then delete (^ prefix)
     #C:\TEMP\BIGFILE.ZIP              Send then truncate (# prefix)
     ~C:\ALREADY\SENT.ZIP              Skip (already sent, ~ prefix)

   OUTBOUND SCAN SEQUENCE
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   For each address with pending mail:
     1. Check semaphore state (recently polled? undialable?)
     2. Check routing rules (direct? via hub? hold?)
     3. Look up in nodelist (phone number, CM flag, IBN host)
     4. Check event restrictions (CM only? listed only?)
     5. Acquire .BSY lock (prevents other nodes from calling same system)
     6. Dial (modem or BinkP via external mailer)
     7. Run session (handshake, send files, receive files)
     8. Release .BSY lock
     9. Record result (success/failure in .try file)
    10. Run tosser if session was successful


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 25

   BSO EXAMPLES
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   You want to send a netmail to 1:234/56 with normal priority:

     File created: OUTBOUND\00EA0038.fut
     (00EA = net 234 in hex, 0038 = node 56 in hex, f = normal, ut = packet)

   You want to poll 1:234/56 immediately:

     QFUTIL /POLL /ADDR:1:234/56
     Creates: OUTBOUND\00EA0038.ilo (zero-length file = poll trigger)

   You want to send a file to 1:234/56:

     QFUTIL /NETMAIL /ADDR:1:234/56 /TO:Sysop /FILE:C:\FILES\UPDATE.ZIP
     Creates: OUTBOUND\00EA0038.flo (contains path to UPDATE.ZIP)


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 26

   QFCONFIG — CONFIGURATION EDITOR
   ============================================================================

   QFCONFIG.EXE is a full-screen TUI program for editing qfront.cfg.
   It provides menus for all configuration sections:

     Main Menu:
       Program Setup          System directories and filenames
       FidoMail Setup         Addresses, nodelist, session settings
       Display Setup          Colors, status messages
       Modem/Dialout          Init strings, COM port, dial settings
       Events                 Event scheduling
       Node Manager           Per-node passwords, conferences
       Area Manager           EchoMail areas, origin lines
       Areafix Setup          Uplinks, forwarding
       File Request Setup     Magic files, paths, limits
       Nodelists              Primary and private nodelists
       Addresses/Aliases      AKA addresses and mappings
       Archivers              ZIP, ARJ, LZH configuration
       NetMail Routing        Routing rules
       Semaphore Files        Trigger files and errorlevels
       Function Keys          F1-F12 key bindings
       External Mail Strings  CONNECT string detection
       Translation/Costing    Phone prefix translations
       Auto Polls             Automatic poll list
       Trashcan               Users to ignore
       Import/Export           AREAS.BBS, FIDONET.NA
       Save Configuration     Write changes to disk

   Navigation:
     UP/DOWN    Select menu item
     ENTER      Open selected item
     ESC        Go back / exit

   In record editors (areas, nodes, routes):
     I          Insert new record
     D          Delete selected record
     ENTER      Edit selected record
     ESC        Return to previous menu


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 27

   QSCAN — ECHOMAIL TOSSER/SCANNER
   ============================================================================

   QSCAN processes FidoNet echomail between .PKT files and your PCBoard
   message base.

   TOSSING (inbound)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QSCAN /TOSS reads .PKT files from the inbound directory, extracts
   messages, and stores them in the appropriate echomail area's message
   base (configured in qfront.cfg as [Area.*] sections).

   For each message:
     1. Read .PKT header (FTS-0001 Type 2 format)
     2. Extract message: from, to, subject, datetime, body
     3. Check for AREA: tag in body → echomail area routing
     4. Check for Areafix robot (messages to AREAFIX/AREAMGR)
     5. Duplicate detection (CRC-32 of from+to+subject+area+MSGID)
     6. Store message in area's .MSG directory
     7. Forward to linked nodes (add SEEN-BY and PATH lines)

   Duplicate messages are silently skipped. The dupe database is stored
   in QDUPES.DAT in the outbound directory (up to 4096 entries).

   SCANNING (outbound)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QSCAN /SCAN reads new messages from your echomail areas (above the
   high water mark) and creates .PKT files for each linked node.

   For each new message:
     1. Read from area's .MSG directory
     2. Add Origin line (if not present)
     3. Add SEEN-BY (our address + linked nodes)
     4. Add PATH (our address)
     5. Create .PKT for each linked node

   After scanning, QSCAN runs the bundle archiver to compress .PKT
   files into FTS-0001 archive bundles (.MO0 through .SU9).


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 28

   QSCAN — AREAFIX
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QSCAN includes a built-in Areafix robot. When a netmail message is
   addressed to AREAFIX, AREAMGR, AREALINK, or ALLFIX, QSCAN processes
   it as an area subscription command.

   The session password is in the Subject line.

   Commands (one per line in the message body):
     +AREANAME       Subscribe to an echomail area
     -AREANAME       Unsubscribe from an echomail area
     +ALL            Subscribe to all available areas
     -ALL            Unsubscribe from all areas
     %LIST           Request list of available areas
     %QUERY          List currently subscribed areas
     %HELP           Request help text (sends AREAFIX.TXT)
     %PAUSE          Pause feed (stop sending but keep subscription)
     %RESUME         Resume paused feed
     %RESCAN         Resend recent messages
     %COMPRESS       Change compression type for bundles

   Unrecognized areas can be forwarded to an uplink node for creation
   (if configured in the Areafix uplinks section).


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 29

   QNLIST — NODELIST COMPILER
   ============================================================================

   QNLIST compiles raw FidoNet nodelists into binary .NDX index files
   for fast address lookup.

   USAGE
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

     QNLIST /COMPILE        Compile all nodelists unconditionally
     QNLIST /COMPILENEW     Compile only if new nodediffs are found

   WHAT IT DOES
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   1. Searches the inbound directory for nodediff archives (.ZIP, .ARJ,
      .LZH, or raw .Znn files).

   2. If found: verifies old nodelist CRC, applies the nodediff, verifies
      new nodelist CRC. On CRC failure, restores the backup.

   3. Compiles the nodelist to a binary .NDX index:
        Each entry: zone(2) + net(2) + node(2) + point(2) + offset(4) + flags(2)
        Flags: bit 0 = Down, bit 1 = Hold, bit 2 = CM, bit 3 = MO, bit 4 = IBN

   4. Processes private nodelists and pointlists (.PVT, .LST, POINTS.*).

   MULTI-NODE SAFETY
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   QNLIST creates a QNLIST.BSY lock file to prevent two instances from
   compiling simultaneously. If you see "QNList appears to be busy
   processing on another node!" and QNLIST is not actually running,
   delete QNLIST.BSY manually.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 30

   QFUTIL — UTILITY PROGRAM
   ============================================================================

   QFUTIL provides command-line access to common FidoNet operations.
   Use it from batch files, DOS prompts, or event scripts.

   CREATE A POLL
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Create a zero-length flow file to trigger QFront to call a node:

     QFUTIL /POLL /ADDR:1:234/56

   This creates an immediate-priority poll (.ilo file in the outbound).

   CREATE A NETMAIL
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Create a .MSG netmail message:

     QFUTIL /NETMAIL /ADDR:1:234/56 /TO:John_Doe /SUBJ:Hello

   With file attach:

     QFUTIL /NETMAIL /ADDR:1:234/56 /TO:John_Doe /FILE:C:\FILES\UPDATE.ZIP

   With message flags:

     QFUTIL /NETMAIL /ADDR:1:234/56 /TO:Sysop /FLAGS:PVT,CRA,K/S

   Available flags: PVT (private), CRA (crash), K/S (kill after send),
   HLD (hold).

   FILE REQUEST
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Request a file from a remote system:

     QFUTIL /FREQ /ADDR:1:234/56 /FILE:NODELIST

   Update request (only send if newer than what we have):

     QFUTIL /UREQUEST /ADDR:1:234/56 /FILE:NODELIST

   With password:

     QFUTIL /FREQ /ADDR:1:234/56 /FILE:SECUREFILE /PWRD:secret


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 31

   FORWARD NETMAIL
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Forward a netmail through another node:

     QFUTIL /FORWARD /ADDR:1:234/56 /TO:John_Doe

   This creates a netmail with the INTRANSIT flag set.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 32

   FILE REQUESTS
   ============================================================================

   QFront supports both inbound (receiving requests from others) and
   outbound (requesting files from others) file requests per FTS-0006.

   INBOUND REQUESTS (others request files from us)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   When a remote system sends a .REQ file during a session:
     1. Each line in the .REQ file is a filename to send
     2. QFront checks magic filenames first (QMAGIC.DAT aliases)
     3. Then searches configured request directories
     4. Sends matched files via Zmodem
     5. Enforces limits from QRLIMIT.DAT (max bytes, max files, min speed)

   OUTBOUND REQUESTS (we request files from others)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Use QFUTIL to create a .req file in the outbound:
     QFUTIL /FREQ /ADDR:1:234/56 /FILE:NODELIST

   QFront sends the .req file during the next session with that node.


   AREAFIX
   ============================================================================

   Areafix is a robot that lets remote sysops subscribe and unsubscribe
   to echomail areas via netmail. Send a netmail to "AREAFIX" at any
   linked node with the session password in the Subject line and commands
   in the body.

   See the QSCAN section for the full list of Areafix commands.


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 33

   (intentionally blank)


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 34

   TROUBLESHOOTING
   ============================================================================

   QFRONT WON'T START
   - Check that qfront.cfg exists and has at least Address= and Outbound=
   - Check COM port number — is the port in use by another program?
   - On DOS: check that DOS4GW.EXE is in the PATH or same directory

   NO MODEM RESPONSE
   - Check COM port number and baud rate in config
   - Try: QFRONT /DEBUG to see modem I/O
   - Check modem cable (must be straight-through, not null modem)
   - Check that DTR is raised (modem won't respond without DTR)
   - On DOS: check FOSSIL driver or 16550 UART detection

   CALLS NOT GOING THROUGH
   - Check RetryDelay — too short causes repeated failures
   - Check routing rules — is the node held or nopoll?
   - Check nodelist — is the node listed as Down or Hold?
   - Check .BSY files in outbound — stale locks block calls
   - On DOS: check modem init string (ATS0=0 disables auto-answer)

   HUMAN CALLERS DON'T GET PCBOARD
   - Check BOARD.BAT errorlevel order (must check 5 before 1)
   - Check that PCBoard is configured for the same COM port
   - Check that QFront is NOT dropping DTR on exit (it shouldn't)

   ECHOMAIL NOT PROCESSING
   - Run QSCAN /TOSS /DEBUG to see what's happening
   - Check [Area.*] sections in qfront.cfg
   - Check inbound directory for .PKT files
   - Check QDUPES.DAT — clear it if dupe database is corrupt

   NODELIST ERRORS
   - Run QNLIST /COMPILE and check for CRC errors
   - If CRC fails: your nodelist and nodediff are out of sync.
     Get a fresh full nodelist and recompile.
   - Delete QNLIST.BSY if it's stale


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 35

   LOG FILE MESSAGES
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Key log messages and what they mean:

   "Waiting for a call"         Normal idle state
   "Ring detected"              Phone is ringing
   "CONNECT at xxxxx bps"       Modem connected, detecting handshake
   "Incoming EMSI"              Remote is an EMSI mailer
   "Incoming YooHoo"            Remote is a YooHoo/WaZOO mailer
   "No mailer handshake"        Human caller, exiting for PCBoard
   "FAX call detected"          FAX tone, hanging up
   "Dialing x:x/x"             Calling a node
   "Establishing FidoMail handshake"  Starting session negotiation
   "End of FidoMail session"    Session completed
   "Successfully sent packet(s)"     Files were transferred
   "Unable to establish initial handshake"  Handshake failed
   "Lost carrier"               Remote hung up during session
   "Caught signal — clean shutdown"  Ctrl-C or kill signal received


                                QFront v1.0.0
                                   Page 36

   FIDONET SPECIFICATIONS USED
   ============================================================================

   QFront v1.0.0 was written clean-room from these public FidoNet
   Technical Standards (FTS) and FidoNet Standards Committee (FSC)
   documents:

   FTS-0001    Basic FidoNet session protocol and .PKT format
   FTS-0004    EchoMail specification
   FTS-0006    YooHoo/WaZOO session handshake
   FTS-5001    Nodelist format and processing
   FTS-5005    Binkley Style Outbound (BSO) directory structure

   FSC-0018    YooHoo hello packet (128-byte structure)
   FSC-0056    EMSI/IEMSI session handshake protocol

   Public Zmodem specification (Chuck Forsberg, Omen Technology)
   Public Xmodem specification (Ward Christensen, 1977)
   Hayes AT command set (standard modem commands)

   No proprietary protocols, no reverse engineering, no decompilation.
   All protocol implementations are from the published specifications.


                                QFront v1.0.0
